## 2. INDIVIDUAL FLOOR EXPLORATION

Each level must be explorable independently.

When a user selects a floor, transition smoothly from the complete building to that floor's architectural floor plan.

Display a furnished, top-down or slightly elevated 3D floor plan. Users should be able to understand the layout and select individual apartments.

### Ground Floor — Bedsitters

Display a realistic architectural arrangement of bedsitter apartments.

Each unit should visually communicate a compact, self-contained living space, including appropriate combinations of:

- Sleeping area.
- Bed and bedside furniture.
- Small living or seating area.
- Kitchenette.
- Bathroom.
- Doors, windows, and circulation space.

### First Floor — One-bedroom apartments

Display individual one-bedroom units with:

- Separate bedroom.
- Living room.
- Kitchen.
- Bathroom.
- Entrance and circulation space.

### Second Floor — Two-bedroom apartments

Display individual two-bedroom layouts with:

- Two separate bedrooms.
- Living room.
- Kitchen.
- Bathroom and any additional spaces appropriate to the layout.

### Third Floor — Three-bedroom apartments

Display individual three-bedroom layouts with:

- Three separate bedrooms.
- Living and dining areas.
- Kitchen.
- Bathroom facilities.
- Entrance and circulation space.

These are illustrative architectural layouts. Make the number of apartments, room arrangements, and dimensions configurable rather than assuming unprovided architectural measurements.

Do not claim the model represents the building's exact construction plans unless actual architectural drawings have been supplied.

### Floor-plan interaction

When users hover over an apartment:
- Highlight its boundaries.
- Display its unit number.
- Show its occupancy status.
- Reveal a small, refined information tooltip.

When users click an apartment:
- Highlight the selected unit.
- Open a unit detail panel or side drawer.
- Show the unit type, floor, tenant, occupancy status, and relevant management information.
- Provide actions to edit the unit, assign a tenant, view the tenant, or update its status.

Use smooth transitions between the complete building, isolated floors, and individual units.

